;; NEGATIVE FIXTURE — must FAIL `--check`. Arc 278, the :then operand wall.
;;
;; A bare non-`?` symbol in a :then value position can never resolve at fire time:
;; `resolve_operand` accepts a literal or a `?var` only. Before the wall this file
;; type-checked CLEAN (arity 1 == :Out's 1 field, and nobody looked inside the argument) and
;; then raised mid-fire, per derived fact, with a Rust `Debug` dump of the AST as its
;; diagnostic.
;;
;; Arc 278 Stone B (DESIGN-STONE-then-is-a-vector-of-singular-facts.md § "Stone B") widened a
;; `:then` value-position operand to admit a fenced EXPRESSION (a call form) — so a nested
;; form like `(:wat::core::+ ?a 1)` is no longer categorically illegal here; this Rust-side
;; wall (`validate_and_reorder_then`) now defers a call-form operand to the wat-side fence
;; (`then-item-fence`, `wat/rete/compile.wat`), which judges it on purity/determinism/composition, not
;; shape. What this wall STILL catches — because it can never resolve under ANY fence, at ANY
;; layer — is a bare non-`?` symbol: not a literal, not a `?var`, not a call. See
;; `probe_arc278_then_user_forms_expr_widening.wat` for the now-legal nested-form sibling of
;; this fixture.
(:wat::core::defrecord :nf::In  [a <- :wat::core::i64])
(:wat::core::defrecord :nf::Out [a <- :wat::core::i64])

(:wat::rete::defrule :nf::compute
  :when  [(:nf::In (?a <- :a))]
  :then  [(:nf::Out bogus-bare-symbol)])

(:wat::core::defn :user::main [] -> :wat::core::nil nil)
